                        "sense_id": "80bbb1cf-9d40-3da7-b92e-b688a1aacb29",
                        "entry_id": "027cb497-e97c-3766-997f-d0052039da21",
                        "glosses": [
                            "vierteljährliche Fastentage zur Heiligung der vier Jahreszeiten (der Mittwoch, Freitag und Samstag nach dem 3. Advents-, dem 1. Fastensonntag, in der Pfingstwoche und nach Kreuzerhöhung)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Katholizismus"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f660f2ea-74c3-4214-a4db-a2658e7d7110",
                                "sense_id": "80bbb1cf-9d40-3da7-b92e-b688a1aacb29",
                                "text": "„Die liturgische Feier der Quatember endete in der Regel mit der Feier einer Vigil.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "516898d5-9efc-49ba-b050-b63cb1f5b0ea",
                        "source_entry_id": "027cb497-e97c-3766-997f-d0052039da21",
                        "source_sense_id": "80bbb1cf-9d40-3da7-b92e-b688a1aacb29",
                        "target_lang_word": "Ember day",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Ember day",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                        "entry_id": "84a13e3d-ba83-3709-8bab-d0f2c082918a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of the three days within the same week, four separate sets of which occur roughly equidistant in the circuit of the year, that are set aside for fasting and prayer. In Western Christianity they are usually the Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday following the first Sunday in Lent, Whitsunday, Holy Cross Day (14 September), and Saint Lucy's Day (13 December)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d945385a-0757-4889-aa2b-967fbf2e2527",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "Days of Faſting or Abſtinence. [...] The Ember-days at the four Seaſons, being the Wedneſday, Friday, and Saturday after #92;#123; the First Sunday in Lent. / The Feaſt of Pentecoſt. / September 14. / December 13."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e9f3d4b-0b90-42b5-a616-75f21ac45669",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "Saints Days, &c. [...] 9 [...] Ember-week [...] 11 [...] Ember-day [...] 12 [...] Ember-day"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4746d289-14c2-49af-bf14-f7186df5912a",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "St. Lambard's day (Auguſt 15) the feaſt of the dedication of Ryerſh church happening frequently in the Ember days (in diebus iiiiᵐ temporum) and alſo in harveſt, the biſhop ordered that it ſhould be kept on the tranſlation of St. Martin in the ſummer, which was July 4th."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1fc179c8-8fe0-4f37-82c8-af7cb6c72378",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "47. When does the third Ember day happen? On the first Wednesday after the Holy Cross. 48. When does that day happen? The Holy Cross always happens on the 14th day of September. 49. Why is that day named Holy Cross? Because that day was instituted in consequence of Queen Helena finding a piece of the Cross on Mount Calvery,^([sic – meaning Calvary]) about the year A.D. 615."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f76de7c9-72d1-4fa4-99ff-0115ef8b4d0d",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "The Ember-days are periodical fasts originally instituted, it is said, by Pope Calixtus, in the third century, for the purpose of imploring the blessing of Heaven on the produce of the earth; and also preparing the clergy for ordination, in imitation of the apostolic practice recorded in the 13th chapter of the Acts."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d48ff65a-6f2e-41fe-8449-8687ef5c3fd5",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "You must not butcher beef on an Ember day but you may cut it up."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e8b72906-a3d4-4c99-971a-5bed98449dcd",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "I then drove to the Red Lion, here I met one of the fathers the first thing he said to me was, I dispense you from eating fish today (an ember day) so I got some meat soup I found the stockport bus, and drive here."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2bd9ce9a-f048-47f9-80ae-787f75fab4c7",
                                                "sense_id": "48629e67-077f-3649-8321-5d21f3aafda4",
                                                "text": "These are the Ember Days, the name derived from the German Quatember, a corruption of quattuor tempore, \"the four times,\" four groups of three days (Wednesday, Friday, and Saturday), marking the seasons by fasting and abstinence."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
